platform_profile: allow writing "custom" to the aggregate sysfs

The aggregate sysfs at /sys/firmware/acpi/platform_profile historically
treated "custom" as informational-only — a sentinel emitted when multiple
platform_profile drivers report conflicting active states. As a result
platform_profile_store() short-circuits with -EINVAL on any write of
"custom", even when the system has a single driver that legitimately
exposes PLATFORM_PROFILE_CUSTOM as a writable choice.

This breaks userspace tools that drive platform_profile via the aggregate
sysfs (TLP, power-profiles-daemon, etc.) on hardware whose vendor driver
uses PLATFORM_PROFILE_CUSTOM as a real selectable profile — most notably
the Lenovo Legion (legion-laptop) driver, where "custom" is the standard
gamezone-aligned name for what older kernels called "balanced-performance".

The check immediately after this one — !test_bit(i, data.aggregate)
returning -EOPNOTSUPP — already prevents writes to drivers that do not
advertise the requested profile. So dropping the special-case rejection
of PLATFORM_PROFILE_CUSTOM does not allow nonsensical writes: it only
allows writes to drivers that have explicitly published "custom" in their
choices bitmap.

Reads at the aggregate level continue to emit "custom" as a conflict
sentinel when multiple drivers disagree — that behavior is separate and
unaffected by this change.

---
 drivers/acpi/platform_profile.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/acpi/platform_profile.c b/drivers/acpi/platform_profile.c
--- a/drivers/acpi/platform_profile.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/platform_profile.c
@@ -419,7 +419,7 @@ static ssize_t platform_profile_store(struct kobject *kobj,

 	/* Scan for a matching profile */
 	i = sysfs_match_string(profile_names, buf);
-	if (i < 0 || i == PLATFORM_PROFILE_CUSTOM)
+	if (i < 0)
 		return -EINVAL;

 	scoped_cond_guard(mutex_intr, return -ERESTARTSYS, &profile_lock) {
